WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.420
Buri kept 16 fruit flies as the parents of every generation.

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.240
He ran 107 bottles.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.200
In every bottle, two alleles of the brown eye colour locus began at the same

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.620
frequency, 0.5. After 19 generations, many bottles held only one allele.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.120
Nobody chose which allele won.

6
00:00:25.083 --> 00:00:26.483
Think of an election poll.

7
00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:30.623
Ask 20 people, and the result can be far from the true vote.

8
00:00:31.503 --> 00:00:32.903
Nobody biased the small poll.

9
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:34.783
It was just small.

10
00:00:35.543 --> 00:00:37.803
Each generation is a poll of the one before.

11
00:00:38.663 --> 00:00:41.983
The offspring carry a random sample of the parents' gene copies.

12
00:00:42.903 --> 00:00:45.823
That chance change in allele frequency is genetic drift.

13
00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:48.223
It needs no difference in fitness.

14
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.707
Gene flow is the movement of alleles between populations.

15
00:00:53.607 --> 00:00:55.747
It pulls their frequencies toward each other.

16
00:00:56.507 --> 00:01:01.187
Say residents carry an allele at 0.6, and immigrants at 0.2.

17
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:06.947
If immigrants are one tenth of the breeding pool, the mixed frequency is 0.56.

18
00:01:07.847 --> 00:01:10.867
That drop of 0.04 is deterministic.

19
00:01:11.747 --> 00:01:13.747
It would happen the same way every time.

20
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:18.907
Whether gene flow helps or harms depends on what the new alleles do in the new

21
00:01:18.907 --> 00:01:19.907
place.

22
00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:26.137
Now sample. The next generation is made by drawing 200 gene copies at random.

23
00:01:26.997 --> 00:01:29.957
The expected frequency stays at 0.56.

24
00:01:31.177 --> 00:01:32.917
Drift adds no push in either direction.

25
00:01:33.737 --> 00:01:37.297
But single outcomes scatter around it, by about 3.5 points.

26
00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:41.477
With only 20 copies, the scatter is about 11 points.

27
00:01:42.257 --> 00:01:45.417
Ten times fewer copies gives about three times the scatter.

28
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.860
Back to Buri's lines.

29
00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.920
Any one line wanders.

30
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.700
Across 107 lines, the frequencies spread toward 0 and 1, while the pooled frequency

31
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.520
showed no systematic change.

32
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.240
Spread without a shift in the mean is the drift prediction.

33
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.340
That fits drift, though it is not proof of it.

34
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.060
Buri also asked how large an ideal population would have to be to spread that fast.

35
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.560
His mean answer was 9.0, not 16.

36
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.080
Buri said the cause was not known.

37
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.440
He judged that variation in how many offspring each fly left, down to complete

38
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.860
sterility, was certainly part of it, and thought crowding for egg laying space

39
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.120
probably played a role.

40
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.700
Either way, chance acted on fewer effective parents.

41
00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.470
Suppose an allele is common on an island and rare on the mainland.

42
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.410
Three explanations make three predictions.

43
00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:44.970
Drift predicts that replicates scatter in both directions, with a mean that stays at

44
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:49.970
the start. Selection predicts that replicates tend to shift the same way on average,

45
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.630
and carriers do better in a fitness test.

46
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.610
Gene flow predicts frequencies that track the amount and source of immigration.

47
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.910
A neutral null is a model of what chance alone would produce.

48
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:03.810
So keep this rule.

49
00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.170
Use replicates, a neutral null, and an independent fitness assay.
